---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SocialShare from "../../components/SocialShare.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Salary Tax Slabs in Pakistan 2026-27: Complete Breakdown"
    description="Salary tax slabs in Pakistan for Tax Year 2027, explained. See FBR rates, a step-by-step calculation, and check your salary slip against the numbers."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <header class="mb-8">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">
                Published: October 11, 2026 · Last Updated: October 24, 2026 ·
                By <a href="/about/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                    >Khuram Shahzad</a
                >
            </p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                Salary Tax Slabs in Pakistan 2026-27: Complete Breakdown
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                Every salaried Pakistani should know how their income tax is
                calculated. This guide breaks down the FBR slabs for Tax Year
                2027 and shows exactly how much you'll take home.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                Every July, when the new tax year begins, the same questions
                come up: "How much tax will I pay this year?" and "Is my
                take-home salary going to change?"
            </p>
            <p>
                The answer depends on your monthly salary and the FBR tax slabs
                for the year. Most people rely on HR or a colleague to explain
                it — but it's actually simple once you know the slabs. This
                guide breaks it down step by step.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Quick Answer: How Salary Tax Works in Pakistan
            </h2>
            <p>
                Pakistan uses a <strong>progressive tax system</strong> for salaried
                individuals. This means your income is divided into slabs, and each
                slab has its own tax rate. The first PKR 600,000 of annual income
                is completely tax-free for Tax Year 2027 (FY 2026-27). Above that,
                tax rates increase from 1% to 35% depending on your income bracket.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                FBR Tax Slabs for Salaried Individuals (Tax Year 2027)
            </h2>
            <p>
                Here are the official slabs for FY 2026-27, as per the Finance
                Act 2026:
            </p>

            <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-50">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Annual Taxable Income (PKR)</th
                            >
                            <th class="text-left p-3 font-semibold border-b"
                                >Tax Rate</th
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">Up to 600,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                ><strong>0%</strong> (Tax-Free)</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">600,001 – 1,200,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >1% of amount exceeding 600,000</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">1,200,001 – 2,200,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >PKR 6,000 + 11% of amount exceeding 1,200,000</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">2,200,001 – 3,200,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >PKR 116,000 + 20% of amount exceeding 2,200,000</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">3,200,001 – 4,100,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >PKR 316,000 + 25% of amount exceeding 3,200,000</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">4,100,001 – 5,600,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >PKR 541,000 + 29% of amount exceeding 4,100,000</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b">5,600,001 – 7,000,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3 border-b"
                                >PKR 976,000 + 32% of amount exceeding 5,600,000</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="p-3">Above 7,000,000</td>
                            <td class="p-3"
                                >PKR 1,424,000 + 35% of amount exceeding
                                7,000,000</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-4">
                <strong>Note:</strong> The 9% surcharge on salaried individuals has
                been abolished for Tax Year 2027. This means your tax is simply the
                slab calculation — no extra surcharge.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Step-by-Step: How to Calculate Your Tax
            </h2>
            <p>
                Let's walk through a real example. Suppose your <strong
                    >monthly gross salary is PKR 150,000</strong
                >. Here's how to calculate your tax.
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 1: Calculate Annual Gross Salary
            </h3>
            <p>
                <code>PKR 150,000 × 12 = PKR 1,800,000</code>
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 2: Subtract EOBI Deduction (If Applicable)
            </h3>
            <p>
                EOBI deduction is <strong>PKR 370/month</strong> (if your salary qualifies).
                <br />
                <code>PKR 370 × 12 = PKR 4,440</code>
            </p>
            <p>
                <code>Taxable Income = 1,800,000 − 4,440 = PKR 1,795,560</code>
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 3: Identify Your Tax Slab
            </h3>
            <p>
                PKR 1,795,560 falls in the slab: <strong
                    >1,200,001 – 2,200,000</strong
                >
                <br />
                Tax formula: <code
                    >PKR 6,000 + 11% of amount exceeding 1,200,000</code
                >
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 4: Calculate Tax
            </h3>
            <p>
                Amount exceeding 1,200,000: <code
                    >1,795,560 − 1,200,000 = 595,560</code
                >
                <br />
                Tax on this amount: <code>595,560 × 11% = 65,512</code>
                <br />
                Total Tax: <code>6,000 + 65,512 = PKR 71,512 (annual)</code>
                <br />
                Monthly Tax: <code>71,512 ÷ 12 = PKR 5,959</code>
            </p>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-3">
                Step 5: Calculate Net Take-Home
            </h3>
            <p>
                <code>Monthly Gross = 150,000</code>
                <br />
                <code>Monthly Tax = 5,959</code>
                <br />
                <code>EOBI = 370</code>
                <br />
                <code>Net Take-Home = 150,000 − 5,959 − 370 = PKR 143,671</code>
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Skip the Manual Calculation — Use Our Free Tool
            </h2>
            <p>
                The example above is simple but real life can get more
                complicated. What if you get a bonus, or your salary changes
                mid-year?
            </p>
            <p>
                Our{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/salary-tax-calculator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    free Salary Tax Calculator
                </a>{" "}
                does all of this instantly. Just enter your monthly salary, and it
                shows:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>Monthly and yearly tax</li>
                <li>
                    Detailed breakdown (gross, EOBI, taxable income, net
                    take-home)
                </li>
                <li>Which slab you fall in</li>
                <li>Tax saving tips</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                It runs entirely in your browser — nothing is uploaded, nothing
                is stored.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Common Mistakes to Avoid
            </h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Forgetting EOBI:</strong> If your salary is under PKR
                    30,000/month, EOBI doesn't apply. If it's above, EOBI is PKR 370/month.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Using the wrong slab:</strong> Many people assume their
                    <em>monthly</em>
                    salary decides the slab. It's actually your <em>annual</em> taxable
                    income.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Not checking the latest slabs:</strong> Tax slabs change
                    every year with the Finance Act. Always use the current year's
                    slabs.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Ignoring tax credits:</strong> Investments in VPS, life
                    insurance, and certain donations can reduce your tax. Talk to
                    a tax professional.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Tax Saving Tips for Salaried Individuals
            </h2>
            <p>
                If your tax bill is high, here are legitimate ways to reduce it:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Voluntary Pension Scheme (VPS):</strong> Contributions
                    are deductible up to certain limits.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Life Insurance Premiums:</strong> Premiums paid for life
                    insurance are eligible for tax credits.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Zakat and Donations:</strong> Payments to approved charitable
                    organizations can provide tax credits under Section 61.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Investment Tax Credit:</strong> Certain investments in
                    shares and mutual funds qualify for credits.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                For personalized advice, always consult a certified tax
                professional.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 mt-3">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Is the 9% surcharge applicable for salaried individuals?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. The 9% surcharge on salaried individuals has been
                        abolished in the Finance Act 2026, effective for Tax
                        Year 2027.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What's the tax-free income limit in Pakistan?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        For Tax Year 2027, the tax-free limit for salaried
                        individuals is PKR 600,000 per year (PKR 50,000 per
                        month).
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        How do I file my income tax return?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        You can file your return online through the FBR's IRIS
                        portal (iris.fbr.gov.pk). It's free but requires
                        registration.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Are bonuses taxed differently?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Bonuses are added to your annual income and taxed at
                        your applicable slab rate. They're not taxed separately.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What if my employer already deducts tax?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Most employers deduct tax monthly and deposit it with
                        FBR. You should still file your annual return to declare
                        income and claim any credits.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p>
                Understanding how your salary tax is calculated gives you
                control over your financial planning. Now that you know the FBR
                slabs and how they apply, you can estimate your take-home salary
                and plan accordingly.
            </p>
            <p>
                For quick calculations, use our{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/salary-tax-calculator/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    free Salary Tax Calculator
                </a>{" "}
                — it does everything in seconds. If you're a student or blogger, you
                might also find our{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/word-counter/"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                >
                    Word Counter
                </a>{" "}
                useful for your writing.
            </p>
            <p
                class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-6 p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200"
            >
                <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This article is for general information
                only, not tax advice. For your specific situation, consult FBR or
                a certified tax professional.
            </p>
        </section>

